DTSTAMP:20261030T060403Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:19900218
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:19900219
SUMMARY:1990 Uzbek Supreme Soviet election (Soviet Union / Russia)
DESCRIPTION:Supreme Soviet elections were held in the Uzbek SSR on 18 Febru
 ary 1990. Of the 500 seats\, 368 were decided in the first round. The resu
 lt was a victory for the Communist Party of Uzbekistan\, which won 348 of 
 the 368 seats in the first round\, whilst around 50 seats went to Unity.\n
 \nElection: Soviet Union / Russia\nSource: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1
 990_Uzbek_Supreme_Soviet_election\nText: Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0)\, via PR
 P (jdpi.pages.dev).
CATEGORIES:Election
URL:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1990_Uzbek_Supreme_Soviet_election
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:0848b75c992ff51a@jdpi.pages.dev
DTSTAMP:20261030T060403Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:19900220
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:19900221
SUMMARY:1990 Canadian federal budget (Canada)
DESCRIPTION:The Canadian federal budget for fiscal year 1990–91 was prese
 nted to the House of Commons of Canada by finance minister Michael Wilson 
 on 20 February 1990. It was the second budget after the 1988 Canadian fede
 ral election. Main estimates were tabled in the House of Commons on 22 Feb
 ruary 1990.\n\nParliament vote: Canada\nSource: https://en.wikipedia.org/w
 iki/1990_Canadian_federal_budget\nText: Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0)\, via PRP
  (jdpi.pages.dev).
CATEGORIES:Parliament vote
URL:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1990_Canadian_federal_budget
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:9c472a6126315f8d@jdpi.pages.dev
DTSTAMP:20261030T060403Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:19900224
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:19900225
SUMMARY:1990 Lithuanian Supreme Soviet election (Soviet Union / Russia)
DESCRIPTION:Supreme Soviet elections were held in the Lithuanian SSR on 24 
 February with run-off elections on 4\, 7\, 8 and 10 March 1990 to elect th
 e 141 members of the Supreme Soviet. In six constituencies\, voter turnout
  was below the required minimum and a third round was held on 17 and 21 Ap
 ril.\n\nElection: Soviet Union / Russia\nSource: https://en.wikipedia.org/
 wiki/1990_Lithuanian_Supreme_Soviet_election\nText: Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.
 0)\, via PRP (jdpi.pages.dev).
CATEGORIES:Election
URL:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1990_Lithuanian_Supreme_Soviet_election
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ce63071dfa0411cb@jdpi.pages.dev
DTSTAMP:20261030T060403Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:19900225
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:19900226
SUMMARY:1990 Nicaraguan general election
DESCRIPTION:General elections were held in Nicaragua on 25 February 1990 to
  elect the President and the members of the National Assembly. The result 
 was a victory for the National Opposition Union (UNO)\, whose presidential
  candidate Violeta Chamorro surprisingly defeated incumbent president Dani
 el Ortega of the Sandinista National Liberation Front (FSLN).\n\nElection:
  Nicaragua\nSource: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1990_Nicaraguan_general_
 election\nText: Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0)\, via PRP (jdpi.pages.dev).
CATEGORIES:Election
URL:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1990_Nicaraguan_general_election
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
