UID:b2e77390b2a58ece@jdpi.pages.dev
DTSTAMP:20261030T060403Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:19900311
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:19900312
SUMMARY:Vytautas Landsbergis takes power (Lithuania)
DESCRIPTION:Vytautas Landsbergis is a Lithuanian politician\, musicologist 
 and former Member of the European Parliament. He was the first Speaker of 
 Reconstituent Seimas of Lithuania after its independence declaration from 
 the Soviet Union.\n\nNew leader: Lithuania\nSource: https://en.wikipedia.o
 rg/wiki/Vytautas_Landsbergis\nText: Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0)\, via PRP (jd
 pi.pages.dev).
CATEGORIES:New leader
URL:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vytautas_Landsbergis
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:0a7a98000f4748c6@jdpi.pages.dev
DTSTAMP:20261030T060403Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:19900313
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:19900314
SUMMARY:1990 Grenadian general election (Grenada)
DESCRIPTION:General elections were held in Grenada on 13 March 1990. The re
 sult was a victory for the National Democratic Congress\, which won seven 
 of the 15 seats. Voter turnout was 68%.\n\nElection: Grenada\nSource: http
 s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1990_Grenadian_general_election\nText: Wikipedia
  (CC BY-SA 4.0)\, via PRP (jdpi.pages.dev).
CATEGORIES:Election
URL:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1990_Grenadian_general_election
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:86d7f21a79fb8e0a@jdpi.pages.dev
DTSTAMP:20261030T060403Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:19900313
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:19900314
SUMMARY:Ertha Pascal-Trouillot takes power (Haiti)
DESCRIPTION:Ertha Pascal-Trouillot is a Haitian politician who served as th
 e provisional President of Haiti for 11 months in 1990 and 1991. She was t
 he first woman in Haitian history to hold that office and the first female
  president of African descent in the Americas.\n\nNew leader: Haiti\nSourc
 e: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ertha_Pascal-Trouillot\nText: Wikipedia (
 CC BY-SA 4.0)\, via PRP (jdpi.pages.dev).
CATEGORIES:New leader
URL:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ertha_Pascal-Trouillot
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:5c809466cb2fee52@jdpi.pages.dev
DTSTAMP:20261030T060403Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:19900314
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:19900315
SUMMARY:1990 Soviet Union presidential election (Soviet Union / Russia)
DESCRIPTION:The 1990 Soviet Union presidential election was held in the Sov
 iet Union on 14 March 1990 to elect a president for a five-year term. This
  was the first and only presidential election to be held in the Soviet Uni
 on. This was due to the office of president being established in 1990\, a 
 year before the dissolution of the Soviet Union in 1991.\n\nElection: Sovi
 et Union / Russia\nSource: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1990_Soviet_Union
 _presidential_election\nText: Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0)\, via PRP (jdpi.pag
 es.dev).
CATEGORIES:Election
URL:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1990_Soviet_Union_presidential_election
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:4f484253e53a2273@jdpi.pages.dev
DTSTAMP:20261030T060403Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:19900318
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:19900319
SUMMARY:1990 East German general election (East Germany)
DESCRIPTION:General elections were held in East Germany on 18 March 1990. T
 hese were the first free elections held in the region since the turbulent 
 Weimar days of 1932 and would become the only truly democratic vote in the
  German Democratic Republic (GDR).\n\nElection: East Germany\nSource: http
 s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1990_East_German_general_election\nText: Wikiped
 ia (CC BY-SA 4.0)\, via PRP (jdpi.pages.dev).
CATEGORIES:Election
URL:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1990_East_German_general_election
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:0b767e6fd0b1002f@jdpi.pages.dev
DTSTAMP:20261030T060403Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:19900318
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:19900319
SUMMARY:1990 Latvian Supreme Soviet election (Soviet Union / Russia)
DESCRIPTION:Parliamentary elections were held in the Latvian SSR on 18 Marc
 h 1990. It was the first free parliamentary election in Latvia since 1931 
 and saw 201 deputies elected to the Supreme Soviet of the Latvian SSR\, 17
 0 of them in the first round. Run-off elections were held on 25 March 1 an
 d 29 April. The Popular Front of Latvia won over two-thirds of the vote.\n
 \nElection: Soviet Union / Russia\nSource: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1
 990_Latvian_Supreme_Soviet_election\nText: Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0)\, via 
 PRP (jdpi.pages.dev).
CATEGORIES:Election
URL:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1990_Latvian_Supreme_Soviet_election
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:f648062fc42eae43@jdpi.pages.dev
DTSTAMP:20261030T060403Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:19900318
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:19900319
SUMMARY:1990 Estonian Supreme Soviet election (Soviet Union / Russia)
DESCRIPTION:Elections to the Supreme Soviet were held in the Estonian SSR o
 n 18 March 1990. Altogether 392 candidates ran for the Soviet-style legisl
 ature's 105 seats\, of which four were pre-allocated to the military distr
 icts of the Soviet Army. The pro-independence Popular Front won the plural
 ity.\n\nElection: Soviet Union / Russia\nSource: https://en.wikipedia.org/
 wiki/1990_Estonian_Supreme_Soviet_election\nText: Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0)
 \, via PRP (jdpi.pages.dev).
CATEGORIES:Election
URL:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1990_Estonian_Supreme_Soviet_election
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
