UID:8780064d5ac4b7d3@jdpi.pages.dev
DTSTAMP:20261030T060403Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:19900324
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:19900325
SUMMARY:1990 Uzbek presidential election (Soviet Union / Russia)
DESCRIPTION:The first and last presidential elections in the history of the
  Uzbekistan Soviet Socialist Republic were held on March 24\, 1990\, one m
 onth after the elections to the Supreme Soviet of the Uzbekistan SSR.\n\nE
 lection: Soviet Union / Russia\nSource: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1990
 _Uzbek_presidential_election\nText: Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0)\, via PRP (jd
 pi.pages.dev).
CATEGORIES:Election
URL:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1990_Uzbek_presidential_election
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:553992b4abb159d2@jdpi.pages.dev
DTSTAMP:20261030T060403Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:19900325
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:19900326
SUMMARY:1990 Hungarian parliamentary election (Hungary)
DESCRIPTION:Parliamentary elections were held in Hungary on 25 March 1990\,
  with a second round of voting taking place in all but five single member 
 constituencies on 8 April. They were the first completely free and competi
 tive elections to be held in the country since 1945\, and only the second 
 completely free elections with universal suffrage in the country's history
 .\n\nElection: Hungary\nSource: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1990_Hungari
 an_parliamentary_election\nText: Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0)\, via PRP (jdpi.
 pages.dev).
CATEGORIES:Election
URL:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1990_Hungarian_parliamentary_election
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:3ceeb14a1a7daafa@jdpi.pages.dev
DTSTAMP:20261030T060403Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:19900325
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:19900326
SUMMARY:1990 Kazakh Supreme Soviet election (Soviet Union / Russia)
DESCRIPTION:Supreme Soviet elections were held in Kazakhstan on 25 March 19
 90. Of the 360 seats\, 270 were directly elected\, whilst 90 were selected
  by public associations. The Communist Party of Kazakhstan won 342 of the 
 360 seats\, with the remainder going to independents. Voter turnout was re
 ported to be 84%.\n\nElection: Soviet Union / Russia\nSource: https://en.w
